-- title:
--   Split places with distinct Artin values above them
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ be a number field, Galois over $\mathbb{Q}$, equipped with a group isomorphism $e$ from $\mathrm{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$ onto $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Z}/3)$, and let $\zeta \in \mathbb{C}$ satisfy $\zeta^4 = -1$. Two subgroups of $\mathrm{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$ are in play: `sylowH e`, consisting of those $\gamma$ whose matrix $e\gamma$ is the entrywise reduction `red` of some member of the $16$-element matrix set `P16`, and `c8H e`, defined in the same way from the $8$-element set `C8`; `fixFld` of each is the corresponding fixed intermediate field of $L/\mathbb{Q}$. For a height-one prime $\mathfrak{P}$ of the ring of integers of `fixFld (c8H e)`, `artinValue e hζ 𝔓` is the value at `seedFrob (c8H e) 𝔓` — the arithmetic Frobenius of $L/\mathbb{Q}$ at a chosen prime of $L$ over $\mathfrak{P}$, raised to the power `relDeg` bringing it into `c8H e` — of the homomorphism `chiGal e hζ` from `c8H e` to $\mathbb{C}^\times$ normalised by $\zeta$ on the distinguished element of order dividing the order of $\zeta$. The assertion: for every finite set $S$ of height-one primes of the ring of integers of `fixFld (sylowH e)` there exist $v \notin S$ and two distinct height-one primes $\mathfrak{P}_1 \neq \mathfrak{P}_2$ of the ring of integers of `fixFld (c8H e)`, each contracting to $v$, with $\mathrm{artinValue}(\mathfrak{P}_1) \neq \mathrm{artinValue}(\mathfrak{P}_2)$.
--
--   This is the non-invariance statement used in Tunnell's octahedral argument: the order-$8$ character of the fixed field of `c8H e` is not fixed by the nontrivial automorphism of that field over the fixed field of `sylowH e`, and this is witnessed at primes of the smaller field outside any prescribed finite set which split in the larger one. It is cited by [`LanglandsTunnell.exists_quadratic_rayClassChar_table_liftTraceSeed_sylowH_of_detDictionaryRow`](thm.html#LanglandsTunnell.exists_quadratic_rayClassChar_table_liftTraceSeed_sylowH_of_detDictionaryRow) in the construction of the quadratic ray class character feeding the induced-character table, and its proof draws on the Frobenius density statement [`FrobeniusDensity.statement`](thm.html#FrobeniusDensity.statement), the identification of the stabiliser of a prime with the cyclic group generated by its arithmetic Frobenius at unramified primes, and [`LanglandsTunnell.exists_finset_forall_inertia_eq_bot`](thm.html#LanglandsTunnell.exists_finset_forall_inertia_eq_bot).
